<a class="ehs-btn ehs-btn--outline ehs-btn--block" href="https://ehsguidelines.net/contact-us/"><svg class="ehs-ic " viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><rect x="3" y="6" width="18" height="12" rx="2"/><path d="M3 8l9 6 9-6"/></svg>Contact</a></div></div><div id="content" class="ehs-content"><a class="skip-link screen-reader-text" href="#content">Skip to content</a><main class="ehs-main ehs-article"><div class="ehs-wrap ehs-article__layout"><div class="ehs-article__col"><header class="ehs-article__head"><div><div class="ehs-breadcrumbs"><nav aria-label="breadcrumbs" class="rank-math-breadcrumb"><p><a href="https://ehsguidelines.net">Home</a><span class="separator"> » </span><span class="last">Eye Injury First Aid: Penetrating Objects, Foreign Bodies and Direct Blows</span></p></nav></div><div class="ehs-article__meta-top"><a class="ehs-chip ehs-chip--health" href="https://ehsguidelines.net/category/first-aid/"><svg class="ehs-ic ehs-chip__ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><rect x="3" y="7" width="18" height="13" rx="2"/><path d="M9 7V5h6v2"/><path d="M12 11v5"/><path d="M9.5 13.5h5"/></svg><span>First Aid</span></a><span class="ehs-badge ehs-badge--glass"><svg class="ehs-ic " viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="8"/><path d="M12 8v4l3 2"/></svg>6 min read</span></div><h1 class="ehs-article__title">Eye Injury First Aid: Penetrating Objects, Foreign Bodies and Direct Blows</h1><div class="ehs-article__by">
<span class="ehs-avatar"><svg class="ehs-ic " viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><circle cx="12" cy="9" r="4"/><path d="M5 20a7 7 0 0 1 14 0"/></svg></span>
<span><strong>Aiman Shazadi</strong> · <time datetime="2026-09-28T09:00:00+00:00">28 Sep 2026</time> · Updated <time datetime="2026-11-01T23:26:08+00:00">1 Nov 2026</time></span></div></div></header><div><figure class="ehs-article__hero"><img fetchpriority="high" width="1013" height="675" src="https://ehsguidelines.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/eye-injuries-penetrating-foreign-bodies.webp" class="attachment-ehs-hero size-ehs-hero wp-post-image" alt="Eye illustration representing eye injury first aid for penetrating objects and foreign bodies" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" srcset="https://ehsguidelines.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/eye-injuries-penetrating-foreign-bodies.webp 1536w, https://ehsguidelines.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/eye-injuries-penetrating-foreign-bodies-300x200.webp 300w, https://ehsguidelines.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/eye-injuries-penetrating-foreign-bodies-1024x683.webp 1024w, https://ehsguidelines.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/eye-injuries-penetrating-foreign-bodies-768x512.webp 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1013px) 100vw, 1013px" /></figure><div class="ehs-prose"><p><strong>Eye injury first aid</strong> is mostly about restraint. The right response to a penetrating object, a foreign body or a blow is usually to protect the eye and get expert help, not to remove, rub or wash. This guide explains the correct steps for each kind of injury and the warning signs that mean an emergency.</p><p>The eye is delicate, and even an injury that looks minor deserves careful handling, since mishandling it in the first few minutes can turn a recoverable injury into permanent vision loss. A surprising number of common first aid instincts, like trying to remove something stuck in the eye, are actually the wrong move.</p><div id="ez-toc-container" class="ez-toc-v2_0_90 counter-hierarchy ez-toc-counter ez-toc-grey ez-toc-container-direction"><div class="ez-toc-title-container"><p class="ez-toc-title" style="cursor:inherit">Table of Contents</p>
<span class="ez-toc-title-toggle"><a href="#" class="ez-toc-pull-right ez-toc-btn ez-toc-btn-xs ez-toc-btn-default ez-toc-toggle" aria-label="Toggle Table of Content"><span class="ez-toc-js-icon-con"><span class=""><span class="eztoc-hide" style="display:none;">Toggle</span><span class="ez-toc-icon-toggle-span"><svg style="fill: #999;color:#999" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="list-377408" width="20px" height="20px" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M6 6H4v2h2V6zm14 0H8v2h12V6zM4 11h2v2H4v-2zm16 0H8v2h12v-2zM4 16h2v2H4v-2zm16 0H8v2h12v-2z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg><svg style="fill: #999;color:#999" class="arrow-unsorted-368013" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="10px" height="10px" viewBox="0 0 24 24" version="1.2" baseProfile="tiny"><path d="M18.2 9.3l-6.2-6.3-6.2 6.3c-.2.2-.3.4-.3.7s.1.5.3.7c.2.2.4.3.7.3h11c.3 0 .5-.1.7-.3.2-.2.3-.5.3-.7s-.1-.5-.3-.7zM5.8 14.7l6.2 6.3 6.2-6.3c.2-.2.3-.5.3-.7s-.1-.5-.3-.7c-.2-.2-.4-.3-.7-.3h-11c-.3 0-.5.1-.7.3-.2.2-.3.5-.3.7s.1.5.3.7z"/></svg></span></span></span></a></span></div><nav><ul class='ez-toc-list ez-toc-list-level-1 ' ><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-1" href="#Eye_Injury_First_Aid_for_Penetrating_Objects" >Eye Injury First Aid for Penetrating Objects</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-2" href="#Eye_Injury_First_Aid_for_Foreign_Bodies" >Eye Injury First Aid for Foreign Bodies</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-3" href="#Eye_Injury_First_Aid_After_Bleeding_or_a_Direct_Blow" >Eye Injury First Aid After Bleeding or a Direct Blow</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-4" href="#Contact_Lenses" >Contact Lenses</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-5" href="#A_General_Principle_Worth_Remembering" >A General Principle Worth Remembering</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-6" href="#When_Eye_Injury_First_Aid_Means_an_Emergency" >When Eye Injury First Aid Means an Emergency</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-7" href="#Preventing_Eye_Injuries_at_Work_and_Home" >Preventing Eye Injuries at Work and Home</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-8" href="#Frequently_Asked_Questions" >Frequently Asked Questions</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-9" href="#References_and_Further_Reading" >References and Further Reading</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-10" href="#Related_Guides" >Related Guides</a></li></ul></nav></div><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Eye_Injury_First_Aid_for_Penetrating_Objects"></span>Eye Injury First Aid for Penetrating Objects<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>When something has penetrated the eye, the contents inside are extremely vulnerable to infection and further damage from any pressure or movement.</p><ul><li>Do not remove the penetrating object under any circumstances.</li><li>Do not force the eyelid open, evert the eyelid, or apply any ointment or eye drops.</li><li>Gently lay the patient flat, with pillow support, and keep the injured eye still.</li><li>Provide emotional support, since patients are often understandably frightened, and reassure them while arranging urgent transport to medical care.</li></ul><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Eye_Injury_First_Aid_for_Foreign_Bodies"></span>Eye Injury First Aid for Foreign Bodies<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>Most foreign bodies in the eye can be removed safely, but the technique matters. Never allow the patient to rub the eye, as this can scratch the cornea and drive the particle in deeper.</p><ol><li>Sit the patient facing a light source and gently look for the particle by asking them to look in different directions.</li><li>If you can see it on the white of the eye or under the lower eyelid, try to lift it away with the corner of a clean, moistened cloth or cotton bud, or gently flush it out with clean water or a balanced saline solution.</li><li>To check under the upper eyelid, gently grasp the lashes, press a cotton bud against the base of the eyelid, and fold the lid back over it to look underneath, then sweep the particle away with a moist cotton bud if visible.</li><li>If the particle sits over the coloured part of the eye, the cornea, only irrigate rather than trying to lift it out directly, since this area is especially easy to scratch.</li></ol><p>If you cannot locate or remove the foreign body after a reasonable attempt, stop trying and seek medical care rather than persisting, since repeated attempts increase the risk of scratching the surface of the eye.</p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Eye_Injury_First_Aid_After_Bleeding_or_a_Direct_Blow"></span>Eye Injury First Aid After Bleeding or a Direct Blow<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>A direct blow, such as from a ball or a squash ball sized object, can cause serious internal damage even without any visible cut, because the eye can be compressed sharply against the bony socket. Sit or lie the patient down, place an unmedicated eye pad gently over the affected eye, secure it with tape, and advise them not to move the eye more than necessary, since eye movement is linked between both eyes and moving the uninjured one can drag the injured one along with it. Seek urgent medical assessment for any closed eye injury from a direct blow, since damage is not always visible from the outside.</p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Contact_Lenses"></span>Contact Lenses<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>Contact lenses can trap chemicals or foreign material against the eye&#8217;s surface, so if a person wearing lenses experiences a chemical injury, remove the lenses yourself if the wearer cannot easily do it themselves, before beginning first aid management. Do not delay treatment trying to remove a lens if it is proving difficult, since getting chemical irrigation started is more urgent.</p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="A_General_Principle_Worth_Remembering"></span>A General Principle Worth Remembering<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>You are only ever one eye injury away from significant, permanent vision impairment. This is not meant to alarm, but to explain why first aid for the eye leans so heavily toward caution: do not remove objects, do not rub, do not apply pressure, and seek medical assessment readily, since the cost of being overly cautious is far lower than the cost of getting it wrong.</p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="When_Eye_Injury_First_Aid_Means_an_Emergency"></span>When Eye Injury First Aid Means an Emergency<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>Call the ambulance or go to hospital straight away if there is:</p><ul><li>An object stuck in the eye, or a wound that has cut the eyeball or eyelid</li><li>Sudden loss or change of vision, double vision or flashes and floaters</li><li>Blood visible inside the eye, or a pupil that looks misshapen</li><li>Severe pain, vomiting or a blow from a high-speed object such as a ball or tool fragment</li></ul><p>Cover both eyes lightly while arranging transport, because the eyes move together. Keep the person calm and still, and give nothing to eat or drink in case surgery is needed.</p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Preventing_Eye_Injuries_at_Work_and_Home"></span>Preventing Eye Injuries at Work and Home<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>Safety glasses, goggles and face shields prevent most serious eye injuries. Use them when grinding, drilling, hammering, spraying, cutting or working with chemicals, and make sure the protection matches the hazard. Good eye injury first aid training and clear emergency procedures reduce the harm when prevention fails.</p><p>In short, good eye injury first aid means protecting the eye, keeping the person still and calling for expert help early. Supervisors should be able to explain eye injury first aid in under a minute, and workplaces should review eye injury first aid steps during safety briefings.</p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Frequently_Asked_Questions"></span>Frequently Asked Questions<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p><strong>Should you try to remove an object that has penetrated the eye?</strong><br />
No. Never remove a penetrating object. Keep the eye still, support the patient, and get them to medical care urgently.</p><p><strong>What should you do if you cannot find or remove a foreign body in someone&#8217;s eye?</strong><br />
Stop attempting removal and seek medical care rather than continuing to try, since repeated attempts risk scratching the eye&#8217;s surface.</p><p><strong>Why should both eyes be kept still after an eye injury?</strong><br />
Eye movements are linked, so moving the uninjured eye also moves the injured one, which can worsen the injury.</p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="References_and_Further_Reading"></span>References and Further Reading<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><ul><li><a href="https://www.aao.org/eye-health/tips-prevention/eye-injury-first-aid" target="_blank" rel="noopener">American Academy of Ophthalmology, Eye Injury First Aid</a></li><li><a href="https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/eye-injuries/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NHS, Eye injuries</a></li><li><a href="https://cpr.heart.org/en/resuscitation-science/2024-first-aid-guidelines" target="_blank" rel="noopener">American Heart Association and American Red Cross, Guidelines for First Aid</a></li></ul><p><strong>Last updated:</strong> November 2026<br />
<strong>Prepared by:</strong> EHS Guidelines Editorial Team</p><p><em>This article is educational and does not replace accredited first aid training or professional medical care. Seek urgent medical assessment for any significant eye injury.</em></p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Related_Guides"></span>Related Guides<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><ul><li><a href="https://ehsguidelines.net/?p=2865">Airway Management: How to Open, Clear and Protect an Airway</a></li><li><a href="https://ehsguidelines.net/?p=2895">Chemical and Heat Burns to the Eye, Welder’s Flash and Snow Blindness</a></li><li><a href="https://ehsguidelines.net/?p=2877">Checking a Pulse: Carotid and Radial Technique Explained</a></li><li><a href="https://ehsguidelines.net/?p=2875">Two Person CPR and How to Change Over Between Rescuers</a></li><li><a href="https://ehsguidelines.net/first-aid-for-wounds-a-wide-ranging-guide/">First Aid for Wounds: A Wide-ranging Guide</a></li><li><a href="https://ehsguidelines.net/first-aid-bleeding-type-treatment-and-precaution/">First Aid: Bleeding Type Treatment and Precaution</a></li></ul></div><aside class="ehs-disclaimer"><svg class="ehs-ic " viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M12 4l9 16H3z"/><path d="M12 10v4"/><path d="M12 17h.01"/></svg><p><strong>Note:</strong> This article is educational guidance. Always follow your site-specific procedures, the current edition of any standard cited, and the instructions of a competent person.</p></aside><nav class="ehs-prevnext" aria-label="Previous and next articles">
